 ],
 "literal": "When the small children of the village wash its white tusk / like the great male elephant bathing in the water-ghat, / you are pleasant, O great lord, to us indeed! But like its / rut that is difficult to approach, / you are fierce, O great lord, to your enemies!",
 "translation": "Like a great bull elephant bathing at the river-ghat, calmly letting the little children of the village wash his white tusks, you are gentle and sweet to us, O great lord! But like that very elephant when in fierce, unapproachable musth, you are terrifying and bitter to your enemies, O great lord!",
 "modern": "ஊரின் சிறு பிள்ளைகள் தன் வெள்ளைக் கொம்புகளைக் கழுவும்போது, நீர்த்துறையில் அமைதியாக மூழ்கிக் கிடக்கும் பெரிய ஆண் யானையைப் போல, எமக்கு நீ இனியவன், பெருமானே! ஆனால், அதே யானையின் அணுக முடியாத மதநீரைப் போல, உன்னைப் பகைப்பவர்க்கு நீ இன்னாதவன் (கொடியவன்), பெருமானே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is addressed to the chieftain Atiyamāṉ Neṭumāṉ Añci by the famous poet Avvaiyār, belonging to the Puram genre under the theme of 'Arasa Vākai' (the greatness of a king). Avvaiyār beautifully contrasts the king's dual nature using a single, powerful metaphor of a war elephant. To his friends, poets, and subjects, Atiyamāṉ is as gentle and accessible as a giant elephant that patiently allows village children to play with it and wash its tusks at the river ghat. However, to his enemies, he is as terrifying and unapproachable as that same elephant when it is in musth (rut), charging with unstoppable fury. The key words are 'iṉiyai' (you are sweet/pleasant) and 'iṉṉāy' (you are bitter/harsh), which perfectly capture the dual facets of a great ruler's personality—benevolent to his people and ruthless to his foes.",
